  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers taper off early this evening Downeast, \nbut persist into Monday over Northern Maine. No flooding \nexpected.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAs of early afternoon Sunday, the center of the low pressure is\nover far SW New Brunswick, with intermittent light to\noccasionally moderate rain showers across the area on the west\nside of the low. Rain totals from this prolonged storm have \nbeen generous, with 0.75 to 1.5 inches in the north, and 1.25 to\n2.5 inches Bangor and Downeast, with even some localized spots \nover coastal Hancock around 3 inches.\n\nAs the low pressure continues to push off to our east/northeast,\nthe trend this evening is for rain showers ending Downeast and \nBangor, but with scattered to numerous light showers persisting \nover Northern Maine. These showers will likely even continue \nthrough the day Monday over Northern Maine (mainly north of\nHoulton) until the center of the departing system gets far\nenough away for drying Monday night. Additional precipitation\nshould be less than 0.25 inches for most areas, though some\nplaces in the north could approach another 0.25 inches through\nlate Monday. Though surface temperatures have been lingering in\nthe upper 30s to low 40s, it has been all rain thanks to\ntemperatures aloft not being any cooler than at the surface. \nHowever, some cooler air aloft finally moves in late this\nafternoon and early this evening, and any time from this \nevening to mid-morning Monday, areas mainly to the north of \nCaribou, in the North Woods, and around Greenville could see a \nfew snowflakes mixing in from time to time. However, think that \nsurface temperatures will be staying a hair above freezing \ntonight and any minor snow accumulations will be limited to \nhigher elevations above where most people live, and road \nconditions should not be impacted. \n\nKey Message 2... Potential for coastal storm development in the\nlater part of next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nIncreasing confidence in a coastal low developing towards the\nend of next week with still a lot of uncertainty regarding this\nsystem. There is a large uncertainty with the models showing a \nnorthern or southerly extent for the track of the system. There \nis potential for snow but high uncertainty on location and \namounts. Potential for gusty winds near the coast depending on \nthe track of the system. Continue to monitor for updates \nregarding this system.",
